From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
Date: Wed, 3 Aug 94 13:44:23 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-32] Information-theoretic death

>In <9408031553.AA08107@media.mit.edu>, Marvin Minsky writes:
>Nancy:
>> >In re whether physical skills are stored in the body: It certainly
>> >*feels* that way, but they might be stored in the brain, but in
>[...]
>> >How could you find out whether kinesthetic skills are partly
>> >stored outside the brain?
>
>Probably by looking for evidence of selective dendrite growth in
>peripheral nerve bundles during learning.

The motor nerves that emerge from the spine via the ventral roots go
straight to the muscles without any dendrites or synapses.   These roots do
contain other nerves that go to nerve cell clusters for the sympathetic and
parasympathetic systems, and these do have some dendrites and synapses.  I
don't know if these learn anything.  Probably though there is some learning
in the spinal cord.

>Two apposite and opposite anecdotals: people who train for a physical
>skill, say tennis, often speak of "muscle memory" and I *guess* that
>this refers to the neuromuscular "grooving" of particular motions.

There's no reason that I know to think that this is other than brain.  We
"localize' lots of mental things in the body -- but that doesn't make it
ture.  Love is not in the heart, nor is courage in the belly, etc.


>part of this that is just muscular development, including whatever
>tuning the peripheral nerves do, would get lost in a rehosting.

I've never heard that there's anything there except muscle size and strength.

> The other is the mental training regimens that some athletes do: intensely
>visualising the activity, and then doing it. Many swear that practice
>time is much better used this way. I used to do this for chess :-)

I do it for piano playing.  I swear it is far more time-efficient.

>Everything is so throroughly connected, though, that it's hard to
>imagine any of this "muscle memory" stuff not being reflected in the
>brain. (I need a good Jerry Lettvin story here, but I can't recall the
>right one).


I'll ask Lettvin, now that he's back in a lab at MIT.




