From: schooley@new-orleans.neosoft.com (John Schooley)
Date: Wed, 3 Aug 1994 12:51:59 -0600
Subject: [#94-8-33] Ritual and Emotion

B. Durant Schoon   writes:

>There was the advent to pschoanalysis to replace religious techniques.  This is
>a form of MENTAL DEBUGGING.
>
>So there are perhaps two routes for the futurist: Change the brain or Placate
>the brain.
>
>1)  Changing the brain would mean just turning off the "mental pain" 
>        (Prozac anyone?) 
>2)  Placating the brain would mean creating modern rituals or mental debugging 
>techniques.
>        (Freudian TechnoRave anyone?)
>
>I'm curious about extropians' attitudes.

Use of ritual may well turn out to be one of  if not the best way to
program and reprogram and access different states of the human mind.  A
general reaction to excesses in religions may well have obscured the
general utility functional techniques that they have used to manipulate the
practitioner's mind.  That we all use various (unconscious) rituals to set
our minds into various states is obvious.  For example, mood music soft
lights, subtle aromas, and Viola!   

That there is an art to use of rituals is certain, that there can be
science of rituals to manipulate the mind is certain in my opinion.  There
should be no feeling of indulging in superstition when we consciously
employ various rituals to manipulate our own minds.  This is a vastly
overlooked and under appreciated area that is available right now to those
who wish to explore various aspects of mind as well as program/deprogram
ourselves.  But first it is necessary to overcome to some degree prejudice
concerning enlightened use of rituals   There may well be a tool of
enormous potential staring us in the face.

John Schooley


